# tests/.unset_whitelist -- subject-test allowlist for tools/scan_unset_placement.tcl
#
# Each entry whitelists a test name (or test-name [string match] glob)
# where one of the cleanup-class commands -- unset, array unset, file
# delete, namespace delete, close, rename -- is the test's subject.
# Placing those commands in `-body` or `-setup` rather than `-cleanup`
# is intentional for these tests; the scanner should not flag them.
#
# Format: one entry per line:
#   <test-name-glob>            <reason>
#
# Lines starting with '#' are comments; blank lines are ignored.  The
# test-name glob is matched via Tcl's [string match].
#
# Verified against the iter #4 audit-loop sampling (3 of 51 confirmed
# subject-tests by inspection) plus the obvious file-by-file rationale:
#
#   tests/unset.tcl, tests/varsystem.tcl, tests/arrayops.tcl test the
#   variable / array commands directly.
#   tests/dict.tcl dict-19.2 verifies R-62849-08308 (dict-update binding
#   unset).
#   tests/newcmds.tcl infovarlinks-* test variable-link cleanup.
#   tests/pendingdelete.tcl pendingdelete-4.1 tests rename-to-empty.
#   tests/coverage/* drives subject vectors in MC/DC test code where
#   the cleanup-class command itself is the line being covered.

# tests/unset.tcl -- the unset command is the entire subject.
unset-*                         unset command itself

# tests/varsystem.tcl -- variable / array unset semantics.
varsystem-4.*                   variable unset semantics
varsystem-6.*                   array unset semantics

# tests/arrayops.tcl -- array unset / single-element unset semantics.
arrayops-5.6                    array unset is the subject
arrayops-5.7a                   unset of array element is the subject

# tests/dict.tcl -- dict update binding unset.
dict-19.2                       dict update binding unset removes key

# tests/newcmds.tcl -- variable-link cleanup tests.
infovarlinks-1.3                variable-link unset is the subject
infovarlinks-1.6                variable-link unset is the subject

# tests/pendingdelete.tcl -- rename-to-empty teardown semantics.
pendingdelete-4.1               rename-to-empty is the test subject

# tests/coverage/ -- MC/DC coverage drivers where the cleanup-class
# command is the line being covered.
coverage2-2.4                   rename of nonexistent command is the subject
coverage5-1.8                   namespace delete with no args is the subject
coverage5-6.*                   array unset MC/DC coverage
coverage8-11.1                  close of bad channel name is the subject
arrsearch-4.2                   array unset mid-search is the subject
core_mcdc-2.1                   unset mid-search drives MC/DC vector
io_misc-2.*                     close with bad args coverage
pa_cov-1.8                      rename-to-empty drives proc-arg cleanup vector
scan_cov-6.3                    scan-leaves-var-unset semantics
scan_cov-8.1                    scan-leaves-var-unset semantics
scandualE-1.1                   scan-leaves-var-unset semantics
secure_setvar_nonsecure-1.1     unset triggers secure setvar nonsecure path
